Boris Johnson's written to every  household in the country today  effectively saying that things are going  to get worse before they get better do  you think a tighter lockdown is  inevitable well the stakes couldn't be  higher this is about saving lives and in  order to save those lives we need to  have an economic response that makes  sure that people are financially secure  in their homes and what the government's  announced so far has been very welcome  but there are a number of gaps and it's  forced many workers and many  self-employed people to choose between  their health and their financial  situation and that's not acceptable so  for example this week we know that Boris  Johnson told us that we needed to stay  in our homes and we should only leave to  get essential provisions to care for  vulnerable relatives or to go to work if  we couldn't work from home and when you  look at the government's guidance on  their website it stated that if you  can't work from home essentially you  should go to work and they've stated but  indeed it's important for business to  carry on now there are workers right  across the country this week you've been  going to construction sites where houses  call centers where their health is being  put at significant risk and the  government needs to now be very clear  about what non-essential and essential  work is and make sure that those workers  are at home if they're not providing an  essential function and that they're  financially protected so just to be  clear then are you effectively calling  for all non-essential workplaces where  work can't be done from home to be  closed down effectively certainly when  we see the pictures that we've seen this  week in warehouses in building sites  where workers are on top of each other  and we've heard reports every single MP  across the country will have had reports  about workers who are frightened because  they know that their colleagues have  gone home sick with the suspected  coronavirus we know that people's lives  are being put at significant risk and  that places further pressure on the NHS  further down the line so there's no  excuse for those employers not to  furlough those staff because the  government scheme is being made  available to provide 80 percent of their  wages but there are a number of other  deficiencies in the government's  economic response certainly in terms of  making sure  that job retention scheme applies to all  workers particularly those who have  reduced hours to make sure that their  full wage is covered there's also an  issue for the self employees now what  the government outlined earlier this  week was welcome  but many self-employed people have to  wait till June to receive a payout from  this and that's not acceptable  when we've seen other countries such as  New Zealand roll out similar schemes far  quicker so we need the government to  really really make sure that resources  are deployed into HMRC  so that those self-employed people who  are eligible can access that cash as  quickly as possible and aren't forced to  go to work there's also a gap in terms  of those self-employed people who aren't  covered by the scheme and when they're  not they're told to go and apply for  Universal Credit Rating statutory SiC  pay which is 94 pounds a week now 94  pounds a week is not enough for anybody  to live on and the government has indeed  admitted that themselves so firstly we  need to have that amount increased so  that it's enough to live on but we also  need the system that can provide the  direct cash immediately end the  five-week Universal Credit wait and  provide all around rather than alone to  those workers and self-employed people  that need to rely on that I just want to  go back to the idea of whether or not  businesses non-essential businesses  should close or not are you worried  about the balance between of course  halting the spread of the virus but also  managing to keep the economy running in  some form particularly if all the  evidence points that we may be in this  situation for many months to come the  most important thing in all of this is  protecting human life and every single  decision that's made economically has to  be centered around that now we will  recover we will need to make sure that  we have a huge investment in support  package available after this crisis is  over to really boost our economy and get  things moving again  but at this time the government's main  priority should be ensuring that people  are able to stay in their homes and that  they're financially protected in order  to do that now Jeremy Corbyn was on the  program last week and he welcomed the  money that had been pledged for  tackling the virus and he then suggested  that it vindicated what he'd done at the  last election and he said it's what I  was condemned for asking for to deal  with the scourge of poverty in Britain  three months ago so do you think that  this kind of emergency financial rescue  package shows that Jeremy Corbyn was  right all along I think the crisis is  highlighted how connected we all are to  each other and it also highlights the  fragility of all of our lives no matter  what our job is whatever our income is  we're one paycheck away from destitution  in many cases and it's also highlighted  the dramatic underfunding of our NHS and  public services to an extent that we  weren't capable of being able to deal  with the crisis such as this so going  forward if we learn anything from this  crisis when we rebuild our economy it  has to be upon foundations that provide  all of us with financial security and in  order to live on and it also provides  the funding that our public services  desperately need to deal with future  crises such as this at the same time  though and do you think it's quite  difficult to compare the arguments that  we were rehearsing in the December  election which seems like a lifetime ago  in a minute with the emergency measures  in the face of the global pandemic that  has perhaps the worst health crisis  since 1918 not at all I mean certainly  what we've always been calling for a  properly funded public services and they  weren't properly funded they're still  not unfortunately and that we would have  been in a better place to tackle this  crisis if we had listened to the advice  of medical advisors three years ago for  example when they told the government  that we needed to invest in protective  equipment advice that was ignored  unfortunately we wouldn't have been in  this position if we provided a safety  net that was capable of catching people  when they fell with it with a benefit  system that was enough for people to  live on and we wouldn't have been in  this situation if we had stronger  security for many of our workforce who  were in extremely precarious situations  such as zero our contract workers and  the self-employed now and the Labour  leadership contest of course on which  you were a contender finishes in a week  fallen away of course down the news  agenda with everything with coronavirus  and but if you win would she be prepared  to join a government of national unity  of the prime minister aren't you - well  I've already been collaborating with the  government and and urging them to listen  to my advice and the advice of my  colleagues in tackling this crisis  because we want to be as helpful as  possible we're not criticizing the  government when we're offering and our  suggestions as to how this crisis can be  better dealt with we're trying to help  and that's what I'll do as leader and  that's what I'll do if I'm not leading  I'm supporting a new leader and now just  one more question that I have to ask you  as well there's been lots of changes of  course to the way that the leadership  contest has run because of coronavirus  and is it right that you've been asked  to record a victory video just in case  and isn't that going to be a little bit  awkward I think it's trying 